Lesson one.

4
00:00:04,626 --> 00:00:05,355
Introduction.

5
00:00:06,470 --> 00:00:07,616
Education only.

6
00:00:08,175 --> 00:00:09,489
Not financial advice.

7
00:00:10,009 --> 00:00:12,061
This is general information and

8
00:00:12,061 --> 00:00:14,264
doesn't consider your objectives,

9
00:00:14,264 --> 00:00:16,164
financial situation or needs.

10
00:00:16,684 --> 00:00:18,448
Crypto is high risk; you could

11
00:00:18,448 --> 00:00:20,139
lose all the money you put in.

12
00:00:20,659 --> 00:00:22,724
Past performance is not a reliable

13
00:00:22,724 --> 00:00:24,434
indicator of future results.

14
00:00:25,484 --> 00:00:27,540
The prices are public historical

15
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,302
daily closes, marked Example.

16
00:00:29,822 --> 00:00:31,889
A close is the last price of the day.

17
00:00:33,044 --> 00:00:34,943
No order is sent from this lesson.

18
00:00:35,942 --> 00:00:37,841
By the end of this series you will

19
00:00:37,841 --> 00:00:40,076
understand what a grid bot does, when it

20
00:00:40,076 --> 00:00:42,310
can capture bounces, and where it loses.

21
00:00:42,772 --> 00:00:44,783
You will know how to plan one, test

22
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:46,682
it on paper, run it, check it, and

23
00:00:46,682 --> 00:00:48,730
change the capital you give to bots

24
00:00:48,730 --> 00:00:50,591
only under written risk rules.

25
00:00:51,111 --> 00:00:52,508
This is education, not

26
00:00:52,508 --> 00:00:53,828
a promise of results.

27
00:00:54,878 --> 00:00:56,219
By the end you will know

28
00:00:56,219 --> 00:00:57,503
what a grid bot places.

29
00:00:58,633 --> 00:01:00,711
You will see that idea on

30
00:01:00,711 --> 00:01:02,686
one real path from 2023.

31
00:01:03,886 --> 00:01:05,395
You will be able to say the

32
00:01:05,395 --> 00:01:06,847
stop rule in one sentence.

33
00:01:07,801 --> 00:01:09,647
When a chart says Example, it

34
00:01:09,647 --> 00:01:11,493
means a real past price used

35
00:01:11,493 --> 00:01:13,339
as an example, not a forecast.

36
00:01:14,389 --> 00:01:16,899
Crypto is digital money, such as bitcoin.

37
00:01:17,419 --> 00:01:19,095
You do not need to have bought

38
00:01:19,095 --> 00:01:20,380
any before this lesson.

39
00:01:20,758 --> 00:01:22,726
An exchange is the website or app

40
00:01:22,726 --> 00:01:25,058
where you swap Australian dollars for

41
00:01:25,058 --> 00:01:27,098
crypto, and crypto back to dollars.

42
00:01:27,618 --> 00:01:29,294
This course does not name one,

43
00:01:29,294 --> 00:01:30,970
and it does not send an order.

44
00:01:31,715 --> 00:01:33,280
Cash is money you can spend.

45
00:01:33,682 --> 00:01:35,581
Here that means dollars, or a coin

46
00:01:35,581 --> 00:01:37,704
that is meant to stay near one dollar.

47
00:01:38,011 --> 00:01:40,203
Coin is the crypto, such as bitcoin.

48
00:01:41,253 --> 00:01:43,599
An order is an instruction to buy or sell.

49
00:01:44,051 --> 00:01:45,858
A buy order swaps cash for coin.

50
00:01:46,378 --> 00:01:48,742
A sell order swaps coin back to cash, and

51
00:01:48,742 --> 00:01:51,106
you can only sell coin you already hold.

52
00:01:52,156 --> 00:01:53,926
A bot is software that places

53
00:01:53,926 --> 00:01:55,548
and refills orders for you.

54
00:01:56,068 --> 00:01:58,433
A grid bot places waiting buy orders

55
00:01:58,433 --> 00:02:00,561
on lines below the price and sell

56
00:02:00,561 --> 00:02:02,689
orders on lines above it, inside a

57
00:02:02,689 --> 00:02:04,423
top and a bottom you choose.

58
00:02:04,943 --> 00:02:06,619
The space between your top and

59
00:02:06,619 --> 00:02:08,351
your bottom is called the band.

60
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,566
The sells need some coin, so a grid

61
00:02:10,566 --> 00:02:12,515
starts with part cash and part

62
00:02:12,515 --> 00:02:14,465
coin, and that is a description,

63
00:02:14,465 --> 00:02:16,336
not advice about what to hold.

64
00:02:17,386 --> 00:02:19,092
A limit order is an order that

65
00:02:19,092 --> 00:02:20,656
waits at a price you choose.

66
00:02:21,176 --> 00:02:22,740
It does not chase the price.

67
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:24,724
Chasing the price would mean

68
00:02:24,724 --> 00:02:26,176
moving the order to follow

69
00:02:26,176 --> 00:02:27,741
wherever the price has gone.

70
00:02:28,590 --> 00:02:30,665
An order fills when the price reaches

71
00:02:30,665 --> 00:02:32,397
its line and the trade happens.

72
00:02:32,858 --> 00:02:35,065
Until then, the order is only waiting.

73
00:02:36,115 --> 00:02:38,070
Think of a ladder leaned on a wall.

74
00:02:38,212 --> 00:02:40,558
The rungs wait, and they do not chase you.

75
00:02:40,834 --> 00:02:43,124
A rung is one price where an order waits.

76
00:02:43,531 --> 00:02:45,710
The gap is the space between two rungs.

77
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:48,043
The ladder is the analogy, and the

78
00:02:48,043 --> 00:02:50,315
chart beside it is the real past path.

79
00:02:51,365 --> 00:02:52,776
The range is the top rung

80
00:02:52,776 --> 00:02:53,905
and the bottom rung.

81
00:02:54,425 --> 00:02:56,548
The levels are the rungs between them.

82
00:02:56,897 --> 00:02:59,188
The stop is the written rule for leaving.

83
00:03:00,037 --> 00:03:01,881
The range is the top line and the

84
00:03:01,881 --> 00:03:03,724
bottom line, taken from the path.

85
00:03:04,203 --> 00:03:07,427
The chart is bitcoin's real daily

86
00:03:07,427 --> 00:03:10,997
closes from 19 July to 16 October 2023.

87
00:03:11,517 --> 00:03:12,969
Every close in this window

88
00:03:12,969 --> 00:03:14,701
stayed between those two lines.

89
00:03:15,487 --> 00:03:18,079
Six equal gaps of about 900 dollars sit

90
00:03:18,079 --> 00:03:20,752
between the bottom line and the top line.

91
00:03:21,272 --> 00:03:23,499
Each gap is about 900 dollars:

92
00:03:23,499 --> 00:03:25,726
the top line minus the bottom

93
00:03:25,726 --> 00:03:27,210
line, divided by six.

94
00:03:27,730 --> 00:03:29,065
It is one step between

95
00:03:29,065 --> 00:03:30,549
lines, not the whole band.

96
00:03:31,069 --> 00:03:32,907
Seven lines make those six gaps.

97
00:03:33,427 --> 00:03:35,215
The levels are the prices on the

98
00:03:35,215 --> 00:03:36,891
rungs where limit orders wait.

99
00:03:37,878 --> 00:03:40,168
Buy orders wait on lines below the price.

100
00:03:40,604 --> 00:03:42,559
Sell orders wait on lines above it.

101
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:45,540
Each fill changes what you hold: a buy

102
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,206
fill adds coin, and a sell fill adds cash.

103
00:03:48,726 --> 00:03:49,843
You choose the band,

104
00:03:49,843 --> 00:03:51,128
the gaps and the money.

105
00:03:51,412 --> 00:03:52,864
The bot places each order.

106
00:03:53,871 --> 00:03:55,664
After a fill, the bot places the

107
00:03:55,664 --> 00:03:57,385
opposite order one rung away.

108
00:03:57,905 --> 00:04:00,083
A buy fill gets a sell one rung higher.

109
00:04:00,470 --> 00:04:02,700
A sell fill gets a buy one rung lower.

110
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:04,840
You still write the stop, and

111
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:06,516
you act when that rule is met.

112
00:04:06,747 --> 00:04:08,982
The bot does not decide the stop for you

113
00:04:08,982 --> 00:04:11,105
unless you turned that setting on, and

114
00:04:11,105 --> 00:04:13,172
you write which one before you start.

115
00:04:13,906 --> 00:04:15,247
Think of a market stall.

116
00:04:15,679 --> 00:04:17,793
The stallholder buys stock a little

117
00:04:17,793 --> 00:04:19,907
cheaper and sells it a little dearer,

118
00:04:19,907 --> 00:04:21,527
and pays a fee on every sale.

119
00:04:21,978 --> 00:04:23,705
A grid can do that while price

120
00:04:23,705 --> 00:04:25,145
bounces inside the band.

121
00:04:25,665 --> 00:04:28,186
A bounce means price moves down and back

122
00:04:28,186 --> 00:04:30,555
up, or up and back down, and stays inside.

123
00:04:31,075 --> 00:04:33,311
One buy at a lower line and the later

124
00:04:33,311 --> 00:04:35,394
sell of that same coin is one pair.

125
00:04:35,914 --> 00:04:38,247
On these daily closes, the summer window

126
00:04:38,247 --> 00:04:40,651
finished only three pairs one rung apart.

127
00:04:41,171 --> 00:04:42,903
The sell line is higher, so the

128
00:04:42,903 --> 00:04:44,747
cash that comes back is more than

129
00:04:44,747 --> 00:04:46,702
the cash that left, before the fee.

130
00:04:46,995 --> 00:04:48,448
A fee is what the exchange

131
00:04:48,448 --> 00:04:50,012
charges when an order fills.

132
00:04:50,193 --> 00:04:51,813
The fee comes out of the gap.

133
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:53,772
If the fees are larger than the

134
00:04:53,772 --> 00:04:55,336
gap, the pair keeps nothing.

135
00:04:55,517 --> 00:04:56,760
This is not a promise.

136
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:59,084
The pair finishes only if price

137
00:04:59,084 --> 00:05:00,957
does bounce and both orders fill.

138
00:05:02,007 --> 00:05:03,236
The daily close is the

139
00:05:03,236 --> 00:05:04,465
last price of the day.

140
00:05:04,886 --> 00:05:06,686
Crypto trades all day, so the

141
00:05:06,686 --> 00:05:08,094
day is cut at a set time.

142
00:05:08,614 --> 00:05:11,138
The daily close is the end of each

143
00:05:11,138 --> 00:05:13,569
UTC day, 11am in Sydney in summer.

144
00:05:14,089 --> 00:05:16,073
This is a simple candle that shows

145
00:05:16,073 --> 00:05:17,748
the day's high, low and close.

146
00:05:18,222 --> 00:05:20,151
Traders call it a candle because

147
00:05:20,151 --> 00:05:22,081
the full version has a thick body

148
00:05:22,081 --> 00:05:23,813
and a thin wick, like a candle.

149
00:05:24,244 --> 00:05:25,753
Here we only need the high,

150
00:05:25,753 --> 00:05:26,982
the low and the close.

151
00:05:27,316 --> 00:05:30,495
This candle is 19 July 2023.

152
00:05:31,015 --> 00:05:32,244
The high, the low, and

153
00:05:32,244 --> 00:05:33,529
the close are labelled.

154
00:05:34,422 --> 00:05:36,551
The example is Bitcoin's public

155
00:05:36,551 --> 00:05:38,207
daily price in US dollars.

156
00:05:38,727 --> 00:05:41,926
From 19 July 2023 to 16 October

157
00:05:41,926 --> 00:05:44,869
2023, every one of those days

158
00:05:44,869 --> 00:05:47,300
closed inside the band.

159
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:49,493
The close crossed the seven

160
00:05:49,493 --> 00:05:51,239
lines 28 times in that window.

161
00:05:51,759 --> 00:05:54,071
The count is an example, not a forecast.

162
00:05:54,591 --> 00:05:56,378
We do not need to read each day.

163
00:05:56,644 --> 00:05:57,987
The shape is the lesson,

164
00:05:57,987 --> 00:05:59,401
and it is marked Example.

165
00:05:59,921 --> 00:06:02,677
Worked check: 19 July

166
00:06:02,677 --> 00:06:05,757
2023 to 16 October 2023.

167
00:06:06,277 --> 00:06:07,786
The low is the bottom line.

168
00:06:08,266 --> 00:06:09,732
The high is the top line.

169
00:06:10,252 --> 00:06:12,095
Nothing on this card is an order.

170
00:06:13,059 --> 00:06:15,993
Those 28 crossings are not 28 results.

171
00:06:16,513 --> 00:06:18,715
A crossing only means the close moved

172
00:06:18,715 --> 00:06:20,782
from one side of a line to the other.

173
00:06:21,224 --> 00:06:22,935
A pair is finished only when a

174
00:06:22,935 --> 00:06:24,789
buy and its later sell have both

175
00:06:24,789 --> 00:06:26,521
filled, and only after the fee.

176
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:28,920
The price can cross many lines and

177
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:30,819
still leave you holding coin if it

178
00:06:30,819 --> 00:06:32,439
walks out through the bottom.

179
00:06:32,976 --> 00:06:35,957
In this example a buy waiting at the

180
00:06:35,957 --> 00:06:39,246
lower of these two lines was reached on

181
00:06:39,246 --> 00:06:42,227
17 August 2023, and the sell one line

182
00:06:42,227 --> 00:06:45,208
above was reached on 29 August 2023.

183
00:06:45,728 --> 00:06:47,516
The card shows the gap, then the

184
00:06:47,516 --> 00:06:49,080
two fees, then what is left.

185
00:06:49,389 --> 00:06:51,512
That is about eight and a half dollars

186
00:06:51,512 --> 00:06:53,244
after fees, for one small pair.

187
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:55,540
It happens only if the price comes

188
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:57,216
back up through the sell line.

189
00:06:57,320 --> 00:06:59,051
If the price leaves through the

190
00:06:59,051 --> 00:07:00,839
bottom, you still hold the coin,

191
00:07:00,839 --> 00:07:02,403
and the pair never finishes.

192
00:07:02,595 --> 00:07:04,383
This card is not money you made.

193
00:07:11,311 --> 00:07:13,099
Now watch what you hold when the

194
00:07:13,099 --> 00:07:15,110
price leaves the band and stays out.

195
00:07:15,373 --> 00:07:16,994
Above the band the sells have

196
00:07:16,994 --> 00:07:18,781
filled and you are holding cash,

197
00:07:18,781 --> 00:07:20,234
with nothing left to sell.

198
00:07:20,638 --> 00:07:22,202
Below the band the buys have

199
00:07:22,202 --> 00:07:23,655
filled and you are holding

200
00:07:23,655 --> 00:07:25,387
coin, with nothing left to buy.

201
00:07:25,669 --> 00:07:27,061
That is why the way down

202
00:07:27,061 --> 00:07:28,820
matters most: every buy fills,

203
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:30,578
and you are left holding coin.

204
00:07:32,628 --> 00:07:34,863
Here is that stop rule on the real path.

205
00:07:35,283 --> 00:07:37,644
It triggers if a daily close is outside

206
00:07:37,644 --> 00:07:39,931
the band, through the top or the bottom,

207
00:07:39,931 --> 00:07:42,440
and the next daily close is still outside.

208
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:44,412
Then the idea is finished.

209
00:07:44,792 --> 00:07:46,886
You stop the bot, which cancels the

210
00:07:46,886 --> 00:07:48,755
waiting orders, and do what you

211
00:07:48,755 --> 00:07:50,849
wrote: keep the coin or swap to cash.

212
00:07:51,369 --> 00:07:53,351
A bot's automatic stop is a price

213
00:07:53,351 --> 00:07:55,039
that can fire during the day.

214
00:07:55,559 --> 00:07:57,258
This course uses the two daily

215
00:07:57,258 --> 00:07:59,094
closes, unless your written plan

216
00:07:59,094 --> 00:08:00,882
says you turned on a price stop.

217
00:08:01,314 --> 00:08:03,447
On 23 October 2023 the close

218
00:08:03,447 --> 00:08:05,859
finished above the top line, and

219
00:08:05,859 --> 00:08:08,178
the next day was still above it.

220
00:08:13,228 --> 00:08:14,681
Through the top, the sells

221
00:08:14,681 --> 00:08:16,357
have filled and you hold cash.

222
00:08:16,466 --> 00:08:18,309
Through the bottom, the buys have

223
00:08:18,309 --> 00:08:20,153
filled and you hold coin that you

224
00:08:20,153 --> 00:08:22,164
bought above where the price is now.

225
00:08:22,523 --> 00:08:23,976
That loss can keep growing

226
00:08:23,976 --> 00:08:25,093
until the stop acts.

227
00:08:25,398 --> 00:08:27,242
You stop the bot either way, then

228
00:08:27,242 --> 00:08:28,806
you follow the written rule.

229
00:08:28,915 --> 00:08:30,703
The bot does not decide the stop

230
00:08:30,703 --> 00:08:32,602
unless you turned that setting on.

231
00:08:32,875 --> 00:08:34,998
EXAMPLE, made up: three buys fill

232
00:08:34,998 --> 00:08:36,794
on the way down, and you paid

233
00:08:36,794 --> 00:08:38,917
about 800 dollars for that coin.

234
00:08:39,437 --> 00:08:41,269
A little below the band

235
00:08:41,269 --> 00:08:42,908
it is worth about 720.

236
00:08:43,428 --> 00:08:45,124
You are down about 80 dollars

237
00:08:45,124 --> 00:08:46,891
before fees, and the loss keeps

238
00:08:46,891 --> 00:08:48,587
growing until your stop acts.

239
00:08:49,637 --> 00:08:51,212
A common mistake is thinking

240
00:08:51,212 --> 00:08:52,721
the bot predicts the price.

241
00:08:53,509 --> 00:08:54,259
It does not.

242
00:08:54,825 --> 00:08:55,607
It only waits.

243
00:08:55,977 --> 00:08:57,486
Another mistake is starting

244
00:08:57,486 --> 00:08:58,659
with no written stop.

245
00:08:59,074 --> 00:09:00,805
There is no promise of results.

246
00:09:01,202 --> 00:09:02,853
The pair card is a teaching

247
00:09:02,853 --> 00:09:04,054
sum, marked Example.

248
00:09:05,104 --> 00:09:07,496
Paper or demo trading is like driving

249
00:09:07,496 --> 00:09:09,580
with L-plates: you practise with

250
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:12,049
pretend money before anything is real.

251
00:09:12,569 --> 00:09:14,086
Lesson 7 shows you how.

252
00:09:15,136 --> 00:09:16,824
This introduction is step one.

253
